  #sec("Section C — Reading Comprehension (Questions 33–50)")

  #block[
    *Read the following passage carefully, then answer questions 33–38.*

    Forests protect the soil. Their roots hold the soil together, and their leaves break the force of falling rain. When people cut down trees without planting new ones, the soil has no protection. Rain washes the loose soil into rivers, and the rivers carry it away. Land that was once rich becomes poor and bare. In time, this land may turn into desert. To prevent this, communities plant new trees and control tree cutting carefully.
  ]

  #pairgrid(
    mcq(33, "According to the passage, what do the roots of trees do?")[
      They break the force of the rain.][They hold the soil together.][They make the soil loose.][They fill the rivers.],
    mcq(34, "According to the passage, what do the leaves of trees do?")[
      They break the force of falling rain.][They make the weather colder.][They help the roots to float.][They stop the rivers from flowing.],
    mcq(35, "What is the main effect of cutting down trees without planting new ones?")[
      The soil is washed away by rain.][The rivers become cleaner.][The birds return to the forest.][The area receives more snow.],
    mcq(36, "The main idea of the passage is that —")[
      planting crops is more important than keeping forests.][forests protect the soil, and cutting them damages the land.][rivers should be filled with soil.][desertification affects only people.],
    mcq(37, "According to the passage, what do communities do to stop land from turning into desert?")[
      They plant new trees.][They pour sand on the fields.][They remove the rivers.][They burn the old trees.],
    mcq(38, "The word 'bare' in the passage most nearly means —")[
      without plants or cover][full of water][full of green grass][extremely steep],
  )

  #block[
    *Read the following passage carefully, then answer questions 39–44.*

    Floods happen when too much water covers land that is usually dry. Heavy and continuous rain is the most common cause. When rain falls faster than the ground and the rivers can absorb it, the water rises and spreads over roads, fields, and houses. Floods can destroy homes and crops, and floodwater often carries diseases. People in flood-prone areas are encouraged to keep important documents in high places, prepare emergency bags, and follow the warnings of their local government.
  ]

